                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain the answer to this question?\n\nProfessor: Sure, which part do you need help with?\n\nStudent: I don't understand what the question is asking.\n\nProfessor: The question is asking you to identify the best way to describe the scenario where a bird eats a brightly-colored insect that tastes bad and then avoids preying on this type of insect again.\n\nStudent: Oh, I see. So, what is the answer?\n\nProfessor: The answer is B) a learned behavior.\n\nStudent: How did you get to that answer?\n\nProfessor: Well, the bird learned from its experience with the brightly-colored insect that it tasted bad and decided to avoid preying on it again. This is an example of a learned behavior, which is something an animal learns through experience rather than being born with it (inherited behavior) or having a physical characteristic that helps it survive (physical characteristic). It is also an adaptation, but that term refers to a trait that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment, rather than a behavior.",
