                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Learned behavior refers to actions or responses that an organism develops through experience or observation.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the definition of learned behavior?\n2. Do any of the options involve actions or responses that an organism develops through experience or observation?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: \nLearned behavior refers to actions or responses that an organism develops through experience or observation. It is not innate or instinctual.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nA) laying eggs to reproduce: This is an innate behavior that is genetically programmed in birds and reptiles.\nB) having hollow, light-weight bones: This is a physical characteristic that is genetically determined and not learned.\nC) avoiding insects that taste bad: This is a learned behavior as it involves the organism learning through experience that certain insects taste bad and should be avoided.\nD) having white feathers on the throat: This is a physical characteristic that is genetically determined and not learned.\n\nAnswering the original question:\nThe option that involves a learned behavior is C) avoiding insects that taste bad.",
