                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Atoms are composed of protons, neutrons, and electrons. The mass of an atom is determined by the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus, while the number of electrons determines the atom's charge.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the atomic number of the atom?\n2. How many protons does the atom have?\n3. How many neutrons does the atom have?\n\nAnswering the sub-questions:\n1. The atomic number of an atom is the number of protons in its nucleus. It is usually denoted by the symbol Z.\n2. The number of protons in an atom is equal to its atomic number (Z).\n3. The number of neutrons in an atom can be calculated by subtracting the atomic number (Z) from the mass number (A), which is the sum of the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus. Therefore, the number of neutrons is A - Z.\n\nAnswering the original question:\nTo calculate the mass of an atom, we need to add the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus. Therefore, the correct answer is B) add the number of protons to the number of neutrons.",
