                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " \n\nFirst, we know that protons and electrons have the same magnitude of charge, but opposite signs. So, D is not correct. \n\nSecond, we know that the mass of a proton is about 1836 times the mass of an electron. So, A is not correct. \n\nThat leaves us with B and C. \n\nNow, we know that kinetic energy is related to the mass and velocity of an object. \n\n$$KE = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$$\n\nSince the mass of a proton is so much greater than the mass of an electron, we would expect the kinetic energy of a proton to be much greater than the kinetic energy of an electron. \n\nHowever, we also know that the velocity of an electron is much greater than the velocity of a proton. \n\nSo, which one cancels out the other? \n\nIt turns out that it's a bit of both. The greater mass of the proton is offset by the greater velocity of the electron, and the two particles have the same kinetic energy. \n\nTherefore, the answer is B.",
