                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what we want to observe in a soil sample. Soil is a complex mixture of minerals, organic matter, water, and air. It also contains living organisms such as bacteria, fungi, and insects. Therefore, we need a tool that can help us observe the different components of soil at a close range.\n\nA) Camera: A camera can capture images of a soil sample, but it may not provide enough detail to observe the different components of soil. Also, a camera may not be able to capture the texture, color, and structure of soil accurately.\n\nB) Compass: A compass is used to determine directions and has no relevance in observing a soil sample.\n\nC) Hand lens: A hand lens, also known as a magnifying glass, is an excellent tool to observe a soil sample. It can magnify the sample up to 10 times, allowing us to see the different components of soil, such as sand, silt, clay, and organic matter. A hand lens can also help us identify soil structure, texture, and color.\n\nD) Telescope: A telescope is not suitable for observing a soil sample as it is designed to observe distant objects in space.\n\nTherefore, the best tool to observe a soil sample is a hand lens or magnifying glass.",
