                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Observing a soil sample requires close examination of its physical properties, such as texture, color, and structure.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the physical properties of a soil sample that need to be observed?\n2. What tools are available for observing these properties?\n3. Which tool is best suited for observing a soil sample?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: The physical properties of a soil sample that need to be observed include texture, color, structure, and the presence of any organic matter or minerals.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2: The tools available for observing these properties include a camera, compass, hand lens, and telescope.\n\nAnswering sub-question 3: The best tool for observing a soil sample is the hand lens. This is because a hand lens provides a magnified view of the soil sample, allowing for a closer examination of its physical properties. A camera may be useful for taking pictures of the sample, but it does not provide the same level of detail as a hand lens. A compass and telescope are not useful for observing soil samples.",
