                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I dissolved a spoonful of sugar in a glass of water. What is the solute, solvent, solution, or suspension in this case?\n\nProfessor: Good question! The sugar is the solute, and the water is the solvent. Together, they form a solution.\n\nStudent: How do we know that it's a solution and not a suspension?\n\nProfessor: A solution is a homogeneous mixture, meaning that the solute is evenly distributed throughout the solvent. In a suspension, the solute particles are larger and will eventually settle to the bottom of the container.\n\nStudent: So, if I let the sugar water sit for a while, would it become a suspension?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. If you let it sit long enough, the sugar particles will settle to the bottom and you'll have a suspension.\n\nStudent: How do we know that the sugar is the solute and not the solvent?\n\nProfessor: The solute is the substance that is being dissolved, while the solvent is the substance that is doing the dissolving. In this case, the sugar is being dissolved by the water, so it's the solute. The water is doing the dissolving, so it's the solvent.\n\nStudent: I think I understand now. Thank you, Professor!\n\nProfessor: You're welcome! Don't hesitate to ask if you have any more questions.",
