                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what dependent and independent variables are?\n\nProfessor: Sure. An independent variable is a variable that is changed or controlled in a scientific experiment to test the effects on the dependent variable. The dependent variable is the variable being tested and measured in an experiment.\n\nStudent: So, in this case, what would be an example of a dependent variable that could be placed along the y-axis?\n\nProfessor: Well, it depends on the experiment or data being analyzed. But generally, the dependent variable is the one that is being measured or observed. For example, if we were studying the growth of plants under different conditions, the height of the plants would be the dependent variable and could be placed along the y-axis.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. And what about negative numbers? Why wouldn't they be placed along the y-axis?\n\nProfessor: Negative numbers can certainly be plotted on the y-axis, but it's not the most common use. The y-axis is typically used for the dependent variable, which is often a quantity that can't be negative, like time or temperature. However, if the dependent variable can be negative, then it can be plotted on the y-axis.\n\nStudent: And what about element symbols? Why wouldn't they be placed along the y-axis?\n\nProfessor: Element symbols are not typically plotted on the y-axis because they are not a quantity that can be measured or observed. They are simply a way to represent different elements in chemistry. The y-axis is usually reserved for variables that can be measured or observed, like the dependent variable.",
                        "answers": [
